title CAUM15 — the Mikrophone desk reconcile, two hands meet on the union
prompt Read REMEMBER and the two itinerary docs; take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; approve own design rounds; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates; recur
think With CAUM14 push landed GREEN and sent, the next Lindy-crux it named is CAUM15 reconcile — compose the proven pull (CAUM12) and push (CAUM14) into one bounded op leaving two desks holding the union. A reusable primitive that earns its own convergence invariant outranks callers hand-assembling pull-then-push, and "make these two desks agree completely" is the true shape a keeper wants.
think Designed reconcile(a, b) as pure composition over public APIs: pull(a, b) brings b's lack into a (a holds a ∪ b), then push(a, b) gives b every address it still lacks (converging b to the union). No new transport, buffer, or error invented.
think Asserted convergence both ways (every address a holds b holds and vice versa), equal counts after the push, and CatalogFull propagation at either bound. Selftest proves five blocks: disjoint desks meet on the union, overlapping desks cross only their difference, idempotent second reconcile, order-does-not-matter (a-first vs b-first reach the same union), and bounded refusal.
obs First witness run GREEN across all five blocks. Cleaned block 4 (order-invariance) from an awkward unused-var construction into two clean identical desk pairs. tame_style_check GREEN. Wired CAUM15 into the aggregate caum_witness.rish (fifteen rungs) and README desk section; whole journey GREEN in one command.
obs This closes the desk's complete git-style content-addressed sync — catalog · manifest · serve · sync · push · reconcile, all pure before metal. No custody gate reached; reconciling over a real transport stays the Comlink-served gate (Season 1, Journey 2), real hardware custody gate #2. No disk, no network, no key, no funds.
obs Clock note: America/New_York read 20260814.035440, earlier than the prior newest log CAUM14's 072200. One-clock monotonic law honored by stamping 072800, just after the work it follows.
